      "text": "The descriptor 'through the air' can be used in a general way to characterize an infectious disease where the main mode of transmission involves the pathogen travelling through the air or being suspended in the air.",
      "exactQuote": "The descriptor ‘through the air’ can be used in a general way to characterize an infectious disease where the main mode of transmission involves the pathogen travelling through the air or being suspended in the air.",
      "blockId": "health-who-terminology-2024-004#block-1"
    },
    {
      "text": "Under the umbrella of 'through the air transmission', two descriptors can be used.",
      "exactQuote": "Under the umbrella of ‘through the air transmission’, two descriptors can be used:",
      "blockId": "health-who-terminology-2024-004#block-1"
    },
    {
      "text": "Airborne transmission or inhalation is used for cases when IRPs are expelled into the air and inhaled by another person.",
      "exactQuote": "Airborne transmission or inhalation, for cases when IRPs are expelled into the air and inhaled by another person.",
      "blockId": "health-who-terminology-2024-004#block-2"
    },
    {
      "text": "Airborne transmission or inhalation can occur at a short or long distance from the infectious person.",
      "exactQuote": "Airborne transmission or inhalation can occur at a short or long distance from the infectious person",
      "blockId": "health-who-terminology-2024-004#block-2"
    },
    {
      "text": "The distance of airborne transmission or inhalation depends on various factors (airflow, humidity, temperature, ventilation etc).",
      "exactQuote": "distance depends on various factors (airflow, humidity, temperature, ventilation etc).",
      "blockId": "health-who-terminology-2024-004#block-2"
    },
    {
      "text": "Direct deposition is used for cases when IRPs are expelled into the air from an infectious person, and are then directly deposited on the exposed mouth, nose or eyes of another person nearby.",
      "exactQuote": "Direct deposition, for cases when IRPs are expelled into the air from an infectious person, and are then directly deposited on the exposed mouth, nose or eyes of another person nearby.",
      "blockId": "health-who-terminology-2024-004#block-2"
    }
  ]
}
